this badger is about to meet its fate  but these people have come not to kill  but to vaccinate it this is a critical  edge or buffer zone in Derbyshire and  this is one of hundreds of badgers in  the county to have been given the jab in  the hope of halting the spread  throughout england of bovine  tuberculosis campaigners say it's by far  the most important weapon in the fight  against this disease actually we're  calling on the government to roll out a  national vaccination programme we've  shown in Derbyshire that at scale we can  operate a vaccination program we have  hundreds of volunteers working with us  we're cost-effective this is the way  forward we don't believe you should be  shooting buggies we believe you can  vaccinate them and in Derbyshire we're  showing the way  not only does wildlife artist Lynn hazel  Smith sculpt badgers  she's also a trained volunteer  vaccinator it's a privilege and a  privilege to be in that position not  many people see badges they often see  them by the roadside dead but to see a  live badger know that you've worked that  hard and that you're going to vaccinate  this budget and you're going to save it  from a chance of contracting TB as well  and to be there with the actual creature  is an utter privilege  the relentless spread of bovine TB is  hit cheshire farmer Phil Latham hard  there is a passion to look after the  animals but there's also the just that  you have your outgoings you've got all  your bills to pay and for no fault of  your own all of a sudden your business  is cut out from underneath you it's very  challenging nationally more than 33  thousand cows were slaughtered last year  because of bovine TB up from almost  twenty seven and a half thousand in 2014  although Badgers are a protected species  more than 19 thousand were cold in 2017  up from ten thousand eight hundred and  eighty six in the previous year  according to the wildlife trust a  further 42,000 could be added this year  are these 100 volunteers in Derbyshire  wasting their time I think they're  making themselves feel good about  themselves I don't think there will be  stopping the disease spreading there's  no paper in the world that shows that  vaccinating Badgers reduces the  prevalence of TB in cuddle the  forthcoming government review will  undoubtedly influence how many more  animals both cows and Badgers will die  in the years to come for now this is one  of the lucky ones Mike McCarthy Sky News
 

